# Keepsake environment variables.
#
# This file documents every var the Worker and the web export read — copy it
# to `.env` (or `apps/api/.dev.vars` for wrangler) and fill in real values for
# local dev. `tools/preflight/preflight.mjs`'s `env-parity` check cross-checks
# the names below (minus the "# secrets:" block and the EXPO_PUBLIC_* names)
# against `apps/api/wrangler.toml`'s top-level `[vars]` and every
# `[env.<name>.vars]` block — the three are required to agree exactly, so a
# var added to one and not the others fails CI before it fails in prod.
#
# Wrangler does not read this file directly: `[vars]` in wrangler.toml is
# what `wrangler dev` and `wrangler deploy` actually use. This file exists so
# there is one human-readable place naming every var and explaining what it's
# for; wrangler.toml's `[vars]`/`[env.*.vars]` blocks restate the same names
# because Wrangler does not inherit `[vars]` into a named environment.

# development | preview | production — read by the Worker to log which
# environment it's running as; not the same as EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV below, which
# is baked into the client bundle instead.
ENVIRONMENT=development
# Overridden per deploy with `wrangler deploy --var COMMIT_SHA:<sha>`; the
# checked-in default is what you see running `wrangler dev` locally, where
# there is no commit to stamp. Read by `/health` and compared against
# `github.sha` by deploy.yml's post-deploy check.
COMMIT_SHA=dev

# --- Moderation budgets (KV counters in apps/api/src/moderation/budget.ts) ---
# Runaway protection, not a product tier: Workers AI meters its own
# allocation regardless of what is set here. These ceilings exist so a
# scraper or a retry loop can't spend the whole day's AI allocation before a
# real giver gets to post.
MOD_TEXT_DAILY_BUDGET=1000
MOD_IMAGE_DAILY_BUDGET=3000
# AI autofill (POST /api/photos/:id/suggest, apps/api/src/suggest/suggest.ts).
# ~15 neurons per call (~$0.17/1000 listings per the plan), so this ceiling
# is set for pure runaway protection, same as the two above — a giver's give
# flow calls this at most a handful of times (once per cover-photo change).
# Its own counter on purpose: it must never share MOD_IMAGE_DAILY_BUDGET,
# since an exhausted image-moderation budget leaves listings pending until
# midnight UTC, and a burst of autofill calls must never be able to starve
# that budget for a real listing trying to get moderated.
SUGGEST_DAILY_BUDGET=1000
# Re-embedding a listing into Vectorize on create/edit (issue #38,
# apps/api/src/search/reembed.ts) for semantic search. Global-only, no
# per-IP twin, since this never runs from its own client-callable endpoint —
# only from inside a listing create/edit this Worker already rate-limits —
# and a failure here falls back to FTS rather than parking anything.
EMBED_DAILY_BUDGET=2000
# Issue #38's kill switch for semantic search (Vectorize + Workers AI
# embeddings). Deployed environments (preview and production) run this at
# "1" as of 2026-09-07 — see wrangler.toml, and
# docs/design/min-score-calibration.md for the measurements behind it.
#
# It stays "0" here, for local development only. Neither Workers AI nor
# Vectorize has a local emulator, so with this on, `wrangler dev` reaches
# real remote services and needs a real CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN — and every
# search you type spends real AI calls against the shared dev index. With it
# off, GET /api/feed?q= is the FTS5 search that works offline. Set it to "1"
# locally only when you specifically mean to exercise the vector path.
#
# Only the literal "1" enables it — see semanticSearchEnabled() in
# apps/api/src/env.ts.
SEMANTIC_SEARCH=0
# Per-IP daily ceilings for the write endpoints. An address is not a person —
# a household or a mobile carrier's CGNAT range sits behind one IP — so these
# are set loose on purpose; they catch a runaway script, not a family posting
# a few keepsakes in a day.
LISTING_IP_DAILY_BUDGET=20
REQUEST_IP_DAILY_BUDGET=20
UPLOAD_IP_DAILY_BUDGET=60
# Issue #92: the hard daily ceilings behind POST /api/listings/:id/reports and
# the two mail-sending routes (POST /api/recover, POST /api/me/email), which
# until then had only the best-effort burst brake in front of them. EMAIL is
# deliberately one shared counter for both mail routes — what's rationed is
# outbound mail, not the path it was requested through.
REPORT_IP_DAILY_BUDGET=10
# Issue #188: per-*recipient* (not per-caller) daily ceiling on the
# "did you ask for this?" confirmation mailed by POST /api/listings/:id/requests.
REQUEST_CONFIRM_ADDRESS_DAILY_BUDGET=3
EMAIL_IP_DAILY_BUDGET=10
# Loose on purpose (see the per-IP note above) — this only stops a single
# retry loop from working through SUGGEST_DAILY_BUDGET alone.
SUGGEST_IP_DAILY_BUDGET=30
# Three independent reporters is enough signal to pull a listing from public
# view pending Kiarash's review; it is not a ban, just a hide.
REPORT_AUTOHIDE_THRESHOLD=3
# Issue #39: minimum approved listings an aspect value needs before
# GET /api/feed/shelves renders it as a shelf. Same in every environment —
# see shelfMinItems() in apps/api/src/env.ts.
SHELF_MIN_ITEMS=3
# 5 MiB matches the client-side resize ceiling in apps/web; this is the
# server-side backstop in case a client is old, broken, or hostile.
MAX_PHOTO_BYTES=5242880
# The `thumb` variant's own, tighter ceiling (768 KiB). A real 640px thumb is
# tens of kilobytes; this is loose enough that no honest encode hits it.
MAX_THUMB_BYTES=786432
# MAX_PHOTOS and IMAGE_TRANSFORMS_ENABLED were removed on 2026-09-06 (launch
# audit): nothing in apps/api/src read either one. The photo limit that is
# actually enforced is `.max(5)` in packages/contracts' CreateListingInput,
# shared with the web client; there was no image-transform code path at all.
# "cf" uses the [[send_email]] binding; "resend" is the fallback behind the
# same sendMail() interface if Email Service enablement or keepsake.ing
# domain onboarding doesn't land in time.
EMAIL_PROVIDER=cf
EMAIL_FROM="Keepsake <hello@keepsake.ing>"
# How much mail may actually leave. "off" sends nothing (local, e2e), "allowlist"
# sends only to EMAIL_ALLOWLIST (preview), "live" sends to real users (production).
# Unset or unrecognised means off — the guard fails closed.
EMAIL_MODE=off
# Comma-separated addresses reachable when EMAIL_MODE=allowlist.
EMAIL_ALLOWLIST=
SITE_URL=http://localhost:8787
# Issue #94: the Cloudflare zone whose cache apps/api/src/lib/photoCache.ts
# purges a deleted photo's two variant URLs from. `caches.default.delete()`
# only reaches the colo executing that request; POST
# /zones/:zone_id/purge_cache reaches every colo, which is the only way to
# close that gap. Not a secret — a zone id is a public identifier (it's in
# docs/runbooks/kiarash-checklist.md) — so it is a var, while the token it
# pairs with (CF_CACHE_PURGE_TOKEN, below) is not. Empty for local dev:
# localhost is in no zone. With either half unset the zone purge is skipped
# silently and the per-colo delete still runs; images.ts's max-age=3600
# bounds how long an unreached copy can outlive the delete.
CF_ZONE_ID=
# Observatory (GET /api/admin/observatory): the account whose Worker
# analytics and logs are read, and the script name to filter them by. Both
# are public identifiers, so both are vars — the token they pair with
# (CF_OBSERVATORY_TOKEN, below) is the credential. Empty for local dev:
# there is no deployed script to read telemetry for, and the Observatory's
# D1-derived half needs none of this.
CF_ACCOUNT_ID=
CF_SCRIPT_NAME=
# Flips the moderation pipeline to canned responses for local dev and
# Playwright, so `give` works without live Workers AI calls.
MOCK_AI=0

# secrets:
# Never committed with real values; set with `wrangler secret put <NAME>
# --env <env>` (see docs/runbooks/deploy.md). tools/preflight's env-parity
# check fails the build if any of these show up in wrangler.toml's [vars] —
# that would commit the secret to the repo in plain text.
TURNSTILE_SECRET=
ADMIN_TOKEN=
RESEND_API_KEY=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Cloudflare API token scoped to Zone.Cache Purge on the CF_ZONE_ID zone
# above (issue #94). Read by apps/api/src/lib/photoCache.ts. A secret, unlike
# its zone id: this one can purge the zone's cache. Deliberately its own
# token rather than reusing CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, which is the CI deploy
# credential and has no business being readable by request-path code.
CF_CACHE_PURGE_TOKEN=
# Read-only Cloudflare API token for the Observatory: `Account Analytics
# Read` + `Workers Observability Read`, nothing else. Its own token rather
# than a reuse of either CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (the CI deploy credential) or
# CF_CACHE_PURGE_TOKEN, on the same principle: request-path code should hold
# the least-powerful credential that does the job, and this one can read
# counters and log lines and write nothing.
CF_OBSERVATORY_TOKEN=
# Where apps/api/src/cron/dailyDigest.ts mails the daily digest. In the
# secrets block rather than the vars above because it is a real person's
# address and wrangler.toml is committed; it is not otherwise sensitive.
ADMIN_DIGEST_TO=

# EXPO_PUBLIC_* — baked into the static web bundle at export time, so these
# are never secrets (anyone can read them out of the shipped JS). Names are
# checked by tools/preflight's public-env check: SECRET|PRIVATE|PASSWORD is
# never allowed, and TOKEN|KEY needs to be on the allowlist in that check.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:8081
EXPO_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon token. Loaded by /consent.js ONLY after the
# visitor grants the analytics category; empty here means nothing loads at all,
# whatever they consented to. On the allowlist above because a beacon token is
# meant to be visible in the page source — that is how the product works.
EXPO_PUBLIC_CF_WEB_ANALYTICS_TOKEN=
# There were three EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ADS_* names here — a tag id and two
# conversion labels. **Removed 2026-09-07, Kiarash's decision: no Google Ads
# for now** (issue #175). They had been declared here and read by
# `apps/web/src/lib/config.ts` since the feature was written, while
# `deploy.yml`'s export step passed none of them, so every preview and
# production bundle inlined all three as `""` and no tag ever loaded. Nothing
# failed and nothing logged, because `verify-public-env.mjs` built its
# expectations from `process.env` — from the very environment that was missing
# them.
#
# That guard now derives its expectations from THIS BLOCK, so a name listed
# here and not passed by `deploy.yml` fails a deployed export. Which means the
# rule is simple: **a name in this block is a promise that the deploy passes
# it.** Do not add one speculatively. If Google Ads is ever revisited, the
# consent plumbing is still here and still tested (`/consent.js`'s advertising
# category, `e2e/consent.spec.ts`) — it is unfed, not removed — and turning it
# back on means re-adding the names here, in `config.ts`, in `deploy.yml`, and
# in the GitHub Environments, together.
EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV=development
